course: physics
lesson: 12
title: Floating in the real world: ice, submarines and balloons
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The rule of floating works for ice, oil, ships and even air: whatever is lighter for its size rises above whatever is heavier for its size.
explain: Water is strange: it grows bigger when it freezes, so ice is lighter for its size than water and floats, which saves fish in frozen ponds.
explain: Air pushes up just like water, only more gently, so a helium balloon or a hot-air balloon floats in the air like wood floats in a pond.
explain: Some tiny things do not float at all but rest on the water's surface, which behaves like a thin stretchy skin: that is how a pond skater walks on water.
fact: Water grows bigger when it freezes, because its molecules line up in an open pattern with gaps, so ice floats, and a full bottle of water left in the freezer can crack.
fact: If ice sank, ponds would freeze from the bottom up and fish could not survive the winter.
fact: About nine tenths of an iceberg is hidden below the sea, which is why ships keep far away, as the Titanic found out in 1912.
fact: A submarine fills tanks with seawater to sink and blows them out with air to rise.
fact: Fish use a similar trick with a little bag of gas inside them, called a swim bladder.
fact: Helium is much lighter than air, so the air pushes a helium balloon up; let go outside, it rises until it pops, and balloons can harm wildlife.
fact: Hot air spreads out and gets lighter for its size than the cold air around it, so a hot-air balloon rises, and sinks slowly as the air inside cools, so the pilot fires a burner to heat it again.
fact: Oil is lighter than water for its size and does not mix with it, so spilled oil spreads over the sea and coats birds' feathers.
fact: A can of normal cola sinks and a can of diet cola floats, because the sugar in normal cola makes it a little heavier.
fact: The surface of water acts like a thin stretchy skin, because water molecules hold tightly together at the top.
fact: A pond skater's light, waxy feet do not break the water's skin, and a drop of washing-up liquid weakens the skin so a resting paper clip sinks at once.
fact: A steel paper clip placed gently rests on the water's skin; it is not floating like a boat, and pushed through the skin it sinks.
fact: Sealed plastic bottles are full of air, so a raft of many bottles can hold up a child, but people on such rafts should still wear life jackets.
exercise: Why do ice cubes float in a glass of water? | Water grows bigger when it freezes, so ice is lighter for its size | Ice is full of tiny air bubbles only | Cold things always float | The glass pushes the ice up
exercise: Why is it lucky for fish that ice floats? | Ponds freeze from the top, leaving water below | Fish can eat the floating ice | Floating ice makes the water warm | Fish can hide on top of the ice
exercise: How much of an iceberg is hidden under the sea? | About nine tenths | About one tenth of it | About half of the iceberg | None of it, it all floats
exercise: Why is the hidden part of an iceberg dangerous? | Ships can hit the huge part they cannot see | It pulls passing ships down to the bottom of the sea | It makes the sea too cold to sail | It melts and floods the ship
exercise: How does a submarine sink? | It fills tanks with seawater | It switches off its lights | It folds in its wings | It lets all its air out of the windows
exercise: How does a submarine come back up? | It blows the water out of its tanks with air | It pedals up to the top using only its engines | It drops its anchor | It waits for a big wave
exercise: What does a fish use instead of tanks? | A swim bladder full of gas | Its fins, which it fills with water | Its gills, which fill up with salt | Its scales, which trap air like a coat
exercise: Why does a helium balloon rise? | Helium is much lighter than air | Helium pulls itself up to the sky | The string makes it light | The ceiling pulls it up
exercise: Why does hot air make a balloon rise? | Hot air is lighter for its size than cold air | Hot air is pushed up by the flame | Hot air pulls the basket up with it by the ropes | The heat makes gravity weaker
exercise: Why does the pilot fire the burner again during a flight? | The air inside cools and the balloon starts to sink | To keep the passengers warm | To make the balloon go faster sideways | To light the way at night
exercise: Why does oil float on top of water? | It is lighter for its size and does not mix | It is heavier than water but too sticky to sink | It is warmer than water | It is pushed up by the glass
exercise: Why does a can of normal cola sink when a diet one floats? | The sugar makes normal cola a little heavier | The diet can is made of a much lighter kind of metal | Diet cola has more bubbles in it | Normal cola is colder inside
exercise: How can a pond skater walk on water? | Its light, waxy feet do not break the water's skin | It swims very fast with its legs | It is lighter than air | It walks on tiny floating leaves
exercise: Is a gently placed paper clip floating like a boat? | No, it rests on the water's skin | Yes, because little bits of steel always float | Yes, it is hollow inside like a boat | No, it hangs from the air above it
exercise: What makes a resting paper clip sink at once? | A drop of washing-up liquid | A pinch of salt sprinkled on top | A cube of ice dropped in next to it | A drop of cold water from the tap
exercise: Why are the bottles on a bottle raft kept closed? | Closed bottles stay full of air and keep floating | Closed bottles are heavier and steadier | Open bottles would float away | The lids make the raft go faster
test: bro just let the balloons go at the party, they'll come back right | No, they rise until they pop and can harm wildlife | Yes, balloons always float back down | Yes, the wind brings them home | No, they float in space for ever
test: What would happen to a pond in winter if ice sank? | It would freeze from the bottom up | It would never freeze at all, however cold it got | It would turn into oil | Fish would float on the ice
test: In a film a whole ship hits a tiny bit of ice and sinks. Why is that not so strange? | Most of the iceberg was hidden under water | Ice is heavier than steel | The ice pulled the ship down with its icy weight | Only the tip of a ship is strong
test: Mia says oil sinks cos it's thick and heavy. right? | No, oil floats because it is lighter for its size | Yes, thick and heavy liquids always sink to the bottom | Yes, oil sinks in the sea | No, oil floats because it is hot
test: You put a can of normal cola and a can of diet cola in a bowl of water. What happens? | The diet one floats and the normal one sinks | Both float on the top, because the cans are full of air | Both sink to the bottom | The normal one floats
test: What would a submarine do if it could not blow its tanks? | It could not come back up | It would rise faster | It would float on its side | It would turn into a boat
test: Why is a helium balloon like a piece of wood in a pond? | Both are lighter than what surrounds them, so they rise | Both are made of plant stuff | Both are heavier than air | Both sink slowly
test: You push a resting paper clip through the water's skin. What happens? | It sinks straight to the bottom | It floats back up to the surface | It stays hanging halfway down the glass | It jumps right out of the water again
test: People crossing a river on a bottle raft should also... | Wear life jackets | Open all the bottles | Fill the bottles with water | Sit on the edge
test: A hot-air balloon pilot stops firing the burner. What happens? | The balloon slowly sinks | The balloon shoots straight up | The balloon pops with a big bang | Nothing at all changes up there
test: Which shows that water grows bigger when it freezes? | A full bottle left in the freezer can crack | Ice cubes melt quickly when you drop them in a warm drink | Snow is white and soft | Rain falls from clouds
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reason for which ice floats upon water? | Water expands upon freezing, so that ice is of lesser density | Ice contains a large quantity of trapped helium | Cold bodies are invariably repelled by water | Ice is supported by the walls of its container
exam: Identify the principal means by which a submarine is caused to descend. | The admission of seawater into its ballast tanks | The extinguishing of its interior lights | The folding of external wings | The release of all the air through its hatches into the sea
exam: It is given that the air within a hot-air balloon is permitted to cool. Ascertain the consequence for the balloon. | It descends gradually | It ascends rapidly | It bursts immediately | It remains at a fixed altitude
exam: Determine the reason for which a steel paper clip, gently placed, remains upon the surface of water. | It is supported by the surface layer of the water | It is of lesser density than water | It is buoyed up by the hollow interior of the steel wire | It is attracted upwards by the air
